Edit your Nginx config to increase the client_max_body_size directive:

Add or update inside the http block:

Or, if you prefer to set it per-site, edit your site config:

Add it inside the server or location block:

Then reload Nginx:

If you're also using PHP-FPM, update /etc/php/8.x/fpm/php.ini:

Then restart PHP-FPM:

root@main:/etc/nginx/sites-available# cd /etc/php/8.4/fpm/php.ini
-bash: cd: /etc/php/8.4/fpm/php.ini: Not a directory
root@main:/etc/nginx/sites-available#

You need to use a text editor, not cd — it's a file, not a directory:

Then find and update these values:

sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/your-site.conf
server {
    client_max_body_size 100M;
    ...
}
Save and restart PHP-FPM:
sudo nano /etc/php/8.4/fpm/php.ini
upload_max_filesize = 100M
post_max_size = 100M
sudo systemctl restart php8.4-fpm



